(window.LEARN_C = window.LEARN_C || {})["mth-12-10"] = {"3.1":{"s":"Integration is the reverse of differentiation: an integral (antiderivative) of f(x) is a function whose derivative is f(x). Since constants differentiate to zero, every answer carries an arbitrary constant c. The integral of a sum or difference is the sum or difference of the integrals, and a constant factor can be taken outside.","f":[{"x":"∫(f(x) + g(x)) dx = ∫f(x) dx + ∫g(x) dx","n":"Sum rule (the difference rule is the same with minus).","r":"Balbharati Maths XII Part 2, p.97"},{"x":"∫k*f(x) dx = k*∫f(x) dx","n":"A constant factor comes outside.","r":"Balbharati Maths XII Part 2, p.97"}],"w":["Always add + c to an indefinite integral.","There is no product rule for integrals: the integral of fg is not (∫f)(∫g)."],"x":["Integration is differentiation run backwards. If F'(x) = f(x), then F is an antiderivative of f, and we write the integral of f(x) dx as F(x) + c. The constant c is there because every constant has derivative zero. So the answer is a whole family of curves, each one a vertical shift of the others. A given point on the curve, such as F(1) = 2, fixes c.","Two rules do most of the work. The integral of a sum or difference is the sum or difference of the integrals, so you can go term by term. A constant factor can be moved outside the integral sign. The power rule is: the integral of xⁿ is x^(n+1)/(n+1) + c for n not equal to −1. For n = −1 the answer is log|x| + c.","Learn the standard list by heart: eˣ, aˣ/log a, sin x, cos x, sec²x, cosec²x, sec x tan x and cosec x cot x. Also know tan x, cot x, sec x and cosec x. When the argument is ax + b, the integral gets a factor 1/a, for example the integral of cos(3x) is (1/3) sin 3x.","Common mistakes: forgetting + c, forgetting the 1/a factor, and using the log rule for 1/x². There is no product rule: the integral of fg is not the product of the integrals. You can always check an answer by differentiating it."],"e":"Integrate 3x² − 4/x + 2 cos x. Go term by term: x³ − 4 log|x| + 2 sin x + c. Differentiate to check: 3x² − 4/x + 2 cos x. Correct."},"3.2":{"s":"In substitution, a part of the integrand is replaced by a new variable t so the integral becomes a standard one; dx is changed using dt = g′(x) dx. It works well when the integrand contains a function and (a multiple of) its derivative. Trigonometric substitutions handle square roots of a² − x², x² + a² and x² − a², and lead to a set of special integrals worth memorising.","f":[{"x":"∫1/(x^2 + a^2) dx = (1/a)*tan^-1(x/a) + c","n":"Special integral.","r":"Balbharati Maths XII Part 2, p.110"},{"x":"∫1/(x^2 - a^2) dx = (1/(2*a))*log((x - a)/(x + a)) + c","n":"Special integral; for a^2 - x^2 the fraction is (a + x)/(a - x).","r":"Balbharati Maths XII Part 2, p.110"},{"x":"∫1/sqrt(a^2 - x^2) dx = sin^-1(x/a) + c","n":"Special integral.","r":"Balbharati Maths XII Part 2, p.110"},{"x":"∫1/sqrt(x^2 + a^2) dx = log(x + sqrt(x^2 + a^2)) + c","n":"Special integral; for x^2 - a^2 change the sign under the root.","r":"Balbharati Maths XII Part 2, p.110"}],"w":["After substituting, change EVERY x, including dx; nothing in x may remain."],"x":["Substitution is the reverse of the chain rule. Choose a part of the integrand, call it t, and replace dx using dt = g'(x) dx. The integral should become a standard one in t. After integrating, put x back. Nothing in x may remain while you work in t, including dx.","Good signs for substitution: a function and (a multiple of) its derivative are both present. Examples: x·e^(x²) with t = x², (log x)²/x with t = log x, and (2x + 3)/(x² + 3x + 5) with t equal to the denominator. A fraction whose numerator is the derivative of the denominator gives log|denominator|.","Linear inside: for f(ax + b), put t = ax + b and the result is F(ax + b)/a. Powers of sine and cosine: if one power is odd, substitute for the other function. For example, the integral of sin³x cos x uses t = sin x.","Four special results come from substitution, and they are worth memorising. The integral of 1/(x² + a²) is (1/a) tan⁻¹(x/a). The integral of 1/√(a² − x²) is sin⁻¹(x/a). The integral of 1/(x² − a²) is (1/2a) log|(x − a)/(x + a)|, and the integral of 1/√(x² + a²) is log(x + √(x² + a²)).","For a quadratic ax² + bx + c in the denominator, complete the square first. Then one of the special results fits. Mistake to avoid: changing the variable but leaving a stray x or dx in the integral."],"e":"Integrate x/(x² + 4). Put t = x² + 4, so dt = 2x dx and x dx = dt/2. The integral becomes (1/2) × (1/t) dt = (1/2) log t. So the answer is (1/2) log(x² + 4) + c."},"3.3":{"s":"Integration by parts handles a product of two functions of different types: keep one as u (to be differentiated) and integrate the other. The choice usually follows LIATE: logarithmic, inverse trigonometric, algebraic, trigonometric, exponential, taking as u the one that comes first. Some integrals, like e^x times a sine, return to themselves and are solved as an equation.","f":[{"x":"∫u*v dx = u*∫v dx - ∫((du/dx)*∫v dx) dx","n":"Integration by parts; u = first function (by LIATE), v = second.","r":"Balbharati Maths XII Part 2, p.128"}],"w":["To integrate log x or tan⁻¹x alone, take 1 as the second function."],"x":["Integration by parts is the reverse of the product rule. For two functions u and v, the integral of u·v dx equals u times the integral of v, minus the integral of (du/dx times the integral of v). Use it when the integrand is a product of two different kinds of function, such as x·eˣ, x·sin x or x·log x.","How to choose u: follow the word LIATE. It stands for Logarithmic, Inverse trigonometric, Algebraic, Trigonometric, Exponential. The function that comes first in this list is taken as u, the other as v. The idea is that u should become simpler when differentiated, and v should be easy to integrate.","To integrate log x or tan⁻¹x alone, write the integrand as 1 times that function and take 1 as v. If the algebraic part is xⁿ, you may need to repeat the method n times, as in x²eˣ. For eˣ times sin x or cos x, apply the method twice. The original integral appears again on the right side, and you solve for it like an equation.","A very useful result: the integral of eˣ(f(x) + f'(x)) is eˣ f(x) + c. Spot it whenever eˣ multiplies a function plus its own derivative. Common mistake: choosing u the wrong way round, which makes the integral harder instead of easier."],"e":"Integrate x·eˣ. Take u = x (algebraic comes before exponential) and v = eˣ. Then the integral is x eˣ minus the integral of eˣ, which is x eˣ − eˣ + c = (x − 1)eˣ + c."},"3.4":{"s":"A rational function f(x)/g(x) is proper when the degree of the numerator is less than that of the denominator; an improper one is first divided out to give a polynomial plus a proper fraction. A proper fraction is then split into partial fractions according to the factors of the denominator: non-repeated linear factors, repeated linear factors, or a linear factor times a quadratic that does not factorise. Each piece integrates to a log or a standard form.","f":[{"x":"(px + q)/((x − a)(x − b)) = A/(x − a) + B/(x − b)","n":"Partial fractions. Also used with repeated and quadratic factors."}],"w":["Divide first if the numerator's degree is not less than the denominator's.","A repeated factor (x − a)² needs two terms: A/(x − a) + B/(x − a)²."],"x":["A rational function is a fraction of two polynomials. It is proper if the degree of the top is less than the degree of the bottom. If it is improper, divide first. You get a polynomial plus a proper fraction, and the polynomial part is easy to integrate.","Then factorise the denominator and split the proper fraction into partial fractions. A linear factor (x − a) gives A/(x − a). A repeated factor (x − a)² gives A/(x − a) + B/(x − a)². A quadratic factor that does not factorise gives (Ax + B)/(quadratic). Write the sum with unknown letters and multiply through by the denominator.","To find the letters, put in convenient values of x (the roots of the factors), or compare coefficients of equal powers of x. Each piece then integrates easily: A/(x − a) gives A log|x − a|, a repeated factor gives a power, and (Ax + B) over a quadratic splits into a log part and a tan⁻¹ part.","Common mistakes: not dividing first when the fraction is improper, and writing a single term for a repeated factor. Always check the letters by putting in one more value of x."],"e":"Integrate 1/(x(x + 1)). Write 1/(x(x + 1)) = A/x + B/(x + 1). Then 1 = A(x + 1) + Bx. Put x = 0: A = 1. Put x = −1: B = −1. So the integral is log|x| − log|x + 1| + c, which is log|x/(x + 1)| + c."}};
